  "author": "groundnuty <groundnuty@gmail.com>",
  "date": "2026-08-07T13:59:05Z",
  "subject": "fix: prune/delete protection for the storage tier's CNPG Clusters",
  "body": "The documented rollback lever (docs/runbooks/bootstrap-cyfronet.md §3,\n'revert the hydrated branch') is a git-only control surface against the\nplatform's hub Argo CD, which runs syncPolicy.automated: {prune: true,\nselfHeal: true}. Neither of the two stateful resources -- the\nlakekeeper-db and mon-data CNPG Cluster objects -- carried any prune\nprotection, so the one procedure meant to make an incident safer would,\nas a side effect, delete both databases: the operator tears the\ninstance down on Cluster deletion and the PVC goes with it via\nownerReference (recoverable only as an orphaned Cinder volume --\ncinder-perf's reclaimPolicy: Retain, confirmed live 2026-08-07 -- via\nmanual PV-rebinding).\n\nAdds argocd.argoproj.io/sync-options: Prune=false,Delete=false to both\nCluster objects only. Prune=false covers a revert dropping the block\nfrom the rendered manifest (normal sync-time pruning); Delete=false\ncovers the separate Application-deletion cascade path (ArgoCD's\nresources-finalizer), which we can't rule out from here since the\nhub's ApplicationSet isn't inspectable. Nothing else in the three\ntiers is stateful -- compute and orchestration render no PVCs/Clusters\nat all, and everything else in storage (ExternalSecrets, the Lakekeeper\nDeployment, PodMonitors) is reproducible from git or the external\nsecret store, so it stays unprotected: blanket Prune=false would turn\nnormal GitOps pruning into permanent litter.\n\nRewrites the rollback section of the tenant runbook to state plainly\nwhat a revert now does and does not remove, and the deliberate second\nstep (drop the annotation + the Cluster block, in its own reviewed\ncommit) required to actually decommission a database. The kind harness\nrenders the identical templates, so the same fence is live there too.\n"
}
